# Forward Signals Between Agents

## Overview

Use `forward` when one input Signal must go to one named child Agent or to the
current parent Agent.

```elixir
route "work.request" do
  forward to: agent(:worker)
end

route "work.completed" do
  forward to: parent()
end
```

Forwarding is part of the route definition. It is not an `Emit` fallback and
does not depend on a channel or a connection search.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Define the Worker

```elixir
defmodule Forward.Work do
  use Jido.Action, name: "forward_work", output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(_input, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | completed: state.completed + 1}}
  end
end

defmodule Forward.Worker do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "forward_worker"

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{completed: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end

  routes do
    route "work.request", Forward.Work do
      state from: output()
    end

    route "work.completed" do
      forward to: parent()
    end
  end
end
```

`work.request` runs an Action. `work.completed` does no local work. It sends
the input Signal to the current parent after commit.

## Define the Parent

```elixir
defmodule Forward.Team do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "forward_team"

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{completed: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end

  routes do
    route "work.request" do
      forward to: agent(:worker)
    end

    route "work.completed", Forward.Work do
      state from: output()
    end
  end

  topology do
    agent :worker, Forward.Worker
  end
end
```

The name in `agent(:worker)` must exist in the same topology. Jido rejects an
unknown name when it validates the definition.

## Portable Definition Data

Forward targets contain only tagged maps and atoms:

```elixir
team = Forward.Team.definition()
worker = Forward.Worker.definition()

{hd(team.routes).handler, List.last(worker.routes).handler}
```

The result is:

```elixir
{
  %{kind: :forward, target: %{kind: :agent, name: :worker}},
  %{kind: :forward, target: %{kind: :parent}}
}
```

The definition contains no PID, registry entry, transport, or dispatch
configuration. Documents and views encode the same target tags.

## Runtime Rules

A successful child forward produces an `AgentSend` runtime Directive. A
successful parent forward produces a `ParentSend` runtime Directive. The
Agent Server runs the Directive after it commits the current Turn.

Forward has these fixed rules:

- `agent(name)` sends only to that declared Agent member;
- `parent()` sends only to the current parent Agent;
- there are no channel targets;
- there is no topology fallback;
- there is no `default_dispatch:` fallback; and
- there is no self-dispatch fallback.

An unknown member makes the definition invalid. An unavailable member returns
`:agent_not_found`. A missing parent returns `:parent_not_found`.

## Forward and Emit

`forward` and `Emit` have different purposes.

| Form | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `route "type" do; forward to: agent(:worker); end` | Route the current input to one declared Agent. |
| `route "type" do; forward to: parent(); end` | Route the current input to the current parent. |
| `emit(signal)` | Publish a new outbound Signal as a generic effect. |
| `reply(signal)` | Return one correlated response to the current requester. |

Routes do not declare output type lists. Connection checks confirm that a
destination accepts a Signal type, but output production stays `:unchecked`.

## Direct Runtime Sends

An Action can request the same direct delivery after commit:

```elixir
Jido.AgentServer.Directive.agent_send(:worker, signal)
Jido.AgentServer.Directive.parent_send(signal)
```

Use a `forward` route when the complete handler is a direct send. Use a
runtime Directive when an Action also changes state or builds a new Signal.

